SMART RAINFALL PREDICTION (GLOBAL WEATHER) - STEP-BY-STEP DOCUMENTATION
======================================================================

Notebook File
-------------
Smart_Rainfall_Prediction_GlobalWeather.ipynb

Purpose
-------
This notebook builds an end-to-end rainfall prediction workflow using global weather observations.
It demonstrates:
1) data transformation,
2) exploratory analytics,
3) sequence modeling with Bidirectional LSTM,
4) model evaluation,
5) MLOps logging with MLflow.


DATASET USED
============
Primary source file: GlobalWeatherRepository.csv (from archive.zip)

Dataset characteristics used in notebook:
- Global station-level weather records
- Raw input fields used: last_updated, location_name, country, latitude, longitude,
  temperature_celsius, humidity, pressure_mb, wind_kph, cloud, precip_mm
- Date coverage seen in transformed output: 2024-05-16 to 2026-03-16
- Transformed records in run: 122,808 rows

Important note:
The raw dataset does NOT contain station_name.
The notebook creates station_name using a formula (see Step 1.5 Formula Cell).


STEP-BY-STEP EXPLANATION
========================

Step 1: Setup and Environment
-----------------------------
Cell purpose:
- Imports all required libraries (pandas, numpy, tensorflow/keras, sklearn, matplotlib, seaborn, plotly, mlflow).
- Configures plotting defaults and suppresses warning noise.
- Prints TensorFlow and MLflow versions for reproducibility trace.

Why this matters:
- Ensures a consistent runtime and dependency baseline before any data/model operation.


Step 1.5: Data Transformation Intro
-----------------------------------
Cell purpose:
- Describes that GlobalWeatherRepository is being transformed to the project schema (`rainfall_data.csv` format).
- Sets expectation that a generated station identifier will be added.


Step 1.5 Formula Cell: station_name Generation
----------------------------------------------
Function: build_station_name(location_name, country, latitude, longitude)

Formula logic:
- Clean `location_name` and `country` by trimming spaces and replacing spaces with underscores.
- Encode latitude tag: absolute value (2 decimals) + N/S suffix.
- Encode longitude tag: absolute value (2 decimals) + E/W suffix.
- Final pattern:
  station_name = "{location}_{country}_{lat_tag}_{lon_tag}"

Example format:
- Abu_Dhabi_United_Arab_Emirates_24.47N_054.37E

Why this matters:
- Creates a stable, unique station key from raw columns when station_name is absent.
- Avoids directly inventing labels not traceable to source fields.


Step 1.5 Main Transformation Cell
---------------------------------
Operations performed:
1) Load raw CSV.
2) Parse timestamp (`last_updated`) and normalize to daily date.
3) Keep only locations with 600+ records (long-enough time series).
4) Remove duplicate location-day rows.
5) Create `station_name` using formula function.
6) Compute dew point approximation:
   Td ≈ T - (100 - RH) / 5
7) Build transformed DataFrame with project schema columns:
   date, station_name, latitude, longitude, region, temperature, humidity,
   pressure, wind_speed, cloud_cover, dew_point, rainfall_mm
8) Add temporal features: month, day_of_year, year.
9) Sort and save to data/raw/rainfall_data.csv.

Output intent:
- Produces model-ready, schema-aligned dataset for downstream training.


Step 2: Data Acquisition and Exploration
----------------------------------------
Cell purpose:
- Reload transformed dataset from data/raw/rainfall_data.csv.
- Validate shape, number of stations, and date range.

Why this matters:
- Confirms transformation output is valid before analytics/modeling.


Step 2.1: Temporal Trend Analysis
---------------------------------
Cell purpose:
- Select one station and plot rainfall over time.

Why this matters:
- Visual sanity-check for seasonality/spikes and data continuity.


Step 2.2: Spatial Analysis
--------------------------
Cell purpose:
- Aggregate average rainfall per station.
- Print top stations by mean rainfall.
- Visualize geospatial rainfall distribution using Plotly map.

Why this matters:
- Reveals spatial heterogeneity and high-rainfall clusters.


Step 3: Preprocessing for Deep Learning
---------------------------------------
Cell purpose:
- Build sliding-window sequences for LSTM.
- Scale features with MinMaxScaler.

Exact settings in notebook:
- Features: temperature, humidity, wind_speed, pressure, rainfall_mm
- Sequence length: 30 days
- Split: 80% train, 20% test (chronological by sequence index)

Why this matters:
- LSTMs require 3D inputs [samples, timesteps, features].
- Scaling stabilizes optimization and improves convergence.


Step 4: Model Training (Bidirectional LSTM)
-------------------------------------------
Model architecture:
- Bidirectional LSTM(64, return_sequences=True)
- BatchNormalization
- Dropout(0.2)
- LSTM(32)
- Dense(32, relu)
- Dense(1) output

Compile/training config:
- Optimizer: Adam
- Loss: MSE
- Metric: MAE
- Epochs: 10
- Batch size: 32
- Validation split: 0.1

MLflow logging:
- Experiment: Rainfall_Prediction_GlobalWeather
- Logs key params: seq_length, units, dataset name, station count, train/test sizes
- Logs test metrics and model artifact

Why this matters:
- Makes training reproducible and traceable for MLOps workflows.


Step 5: Evaluation and Decision Support
---------------------------------------
Cell purpose:
- Generate predictions on test set.
- Compute MSE, RMSE, MAE.
- Plot actual vs predicted time series.
- Plot scatter (actual vs predicted) with diagonal reference.

Why this matters:
- Quantifies error and visually checks calibration/bias.
- Supports decision-support confidence discussion.


Step 6: MLOps Architecture Notes
--------------------------------
Notebook also documents:
- DVC-based data versioning workflow,
- MLflow experiment tracking,
- FastAPI serving pattern,
- Dockerized deployment concept.


Step 7: Conclusion
------------------
The notebook concludes with:
- successful end-to-end run on transformed GlobalWeatherRepository data,
- recorded test metrics,
- logged MLflow experiment for reproducibility.


FILES PRODUCED/USED DURING PIPELINE
===================================
Inputs:
- GlobalWeatherRepository.csv

Outputs:
- data/raw/rainfall_data.csv (transformed schema)
- MLflow run artifacts/metrics under experiment Rainfall_Prediction_GlobalWeather

Intermediate objects (in memory):
- gwr, gwr_f, df, X_train, X_test, y_train, y_test, model, predictions


ASSUMPTIONS & LIMITATIONS
=========================
1) station_name is generated (not native in source dataset).
2) Dew point is approximated formula-based, not directly measured in raw input.
3) The sequence model is trained on one selected station series (first station in current ordering).
4) Metrics reported are on scaled target space unless inverse transform is explicitly applied.


HOW TO EXPLAIN TO REVIEWER/FACULTY
==================================
- Dataset origin: GlobalWeatherRepository.csv (archive.zip).
- Schema gap handling: station_name created by deterministic formula from source columns.
- Pipeline integrity: transformation -> EDA -> sequence prep -> BiLSTM training -> evaluation.
- Reproducibility: MLflow logs and saved transformed dataset enable reruns and auditability.
